<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.10.1: The Vertex and Intercepts of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02498</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.10.1: The Vertex and Intercepts of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>83cad1cb-379b-4ecf-9269-e4ab1bbb1913</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">These expressions each define the same function.</para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>\(x^2+6x+8\)</item>
        <item>\((x+2)(x+4)\)</item>
        <item>\((x+3)^2−1\)</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00002">Without graphing or doing any calculations, determine where the following features would be on a graph that represents the function.</para>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <exercise id="_6f6efa7e-aa7e-4569-8a0f-891502cd7b1b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Enter the \(x\)-coordinate.</para>
            <para id="para-00004">The \(x\)-coordinate of the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">vertex (of a graph)</term>.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is -3.</para>
            <para id="para-00006">The correct answer is -3. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is -3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6201a064-001a-4537-9c4a-848ca8f0cc73" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Enter the \(y\)-coordinate.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex (of a graph).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is -1.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">The correct answer is -1. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is -1.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <exercise id="_380ada69-6116-46ec-b904-b6ac6d41ea2f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011"><emphasis>Select both</emphasis> <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">\(x\)-intercepts</term>.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00012">Correct! The \(x\)-intercepts are \((-2,0)\) and \((-4,0)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00013">The correct answers are: \((-2,0)\), \((-4,0)\). The \(x\)-intercepts are \((-2,0)\) and \((-4,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <exercise id="_8dd03ced-8b05-43a1-b1e7-bf429fcbcb0b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00014">Enter the \(y\)-coordinate of the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para id="para-00015">The \(y\)-coordinate of the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">\(y\)-intercept</term> \((0, \_\_\_\_\_)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00016">Correct! The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,8)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The correct answer is 8. The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,8)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>